1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:07,680
Say it's ten in the morning on a Tuesday, and you open your banking app to pay two bills of 20 million dong each.

2
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:11,530
The first goes to a supplier at the exact same bank as you.

3
00:00:11,530 --> 00:00:15,190
The second goes to a vendor across town at a rival bank.

4
00:00:15,190 --> 00:00:20,550
Three seconds later, both phones light up with a plus twenty million notification.

5
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:25,710
Both transfers cost zero fees, and both partners can spend the cash right away.

6
00:00:25,710 --> 00:00:28,190
On glass, the two taps look identical.

7
00:00:28,190 --> 00:00:41,260
Underneath, those two 20-million-dong payments just took opposite roads through the banking system — one staying inside a single room, and the other crossing a national switch toward the central bank.

8
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:51,480
Take the first transfer: sending 20 million dong to someone at your own bank, which bankers call an On-Us transaction.

9
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:56,200
What most people forget is what that balance means to the bank.

10
00:00:56,200 --> 00:01:00,390
To you, twenty million dong in checking is cash you own.

11
00:01:00,390 --> 00:01:06,380
To the bank, it's a demand deposit liability — a debt the bank owes you on call.

12
00:01:06,380 --> 00:01:14,170
Because both you and your vendor keep accounts under one roof, your bank keeps the books for both sides.

13
00:01:14,170 --> 00:01:19,710
The moment you tap send, its core database changes two rows on one ledger.

14
00:01:19,710 --> 00:01:22,710
Your row drops by twenty million dong...

15
00:01:22,710 --> 00:01:26,230
and your vendor's row climbs by twenty million.

16
00:01:26,230 --> 00:01:31,620
Not a single dong of central-bank reserve cash ever leaves the building.

17
00:01:32,850 --> 00:01:37,090
Look at the bank's own cash pool right after that On-Us click.

18
00:01:37,090 --> 00:01:43,450
Before you tapped send, the bank held twenty million dong in demand deposits under your name.

19
00:01:43,450 --> 00:01:52,680
After the click, it still holds that exact same twenty million dong — it just swapped the name on its IOU from yours to your partner's.

20
00:01:52,680 --> 00:02:01,020
In an example banking setup paying around 0.1% a year on checking balances, the bank's cheap CASA pool moves by zero dong.

21
00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:03,410
Not a single cent leaks to a rival.

22
00:02:03,410 --> 00:02:17,540
Meanwhile, on your company books under Circular 99 of 2025, you clear your supplier bill cleanly: Debit Trade Payables on Account 331 for 20 million, and Credit Demand Deposits on Account 112 for 20 million.

23
00:02:19,090 --> 00:02:27,730
Now trace the second transfer: sending twenty million dong to a vendor at a completely different bank, known as an Off-Us transfer.

24
00:02:27,730 --> 00:02:29,450
Here you hit a legal wall.

25
00:02:29,450 --> 00:02:37,230
Your bank has zero authority to open a competitor's database and type plus twenty million onto someone else's balance.

26
00:02:37,230 --> 00:02:44,490
And the receiving bank won't hand out real spendable cash on a Saturday night just on a friendly text message.

27
00:02:44,490 --> 00:02:51,940
If the receiving bank pays out early and your bank fails before Monday morning, the receiving bank eats the loss.

28
00:02:51,940 --> 00:03:00,450
Yet even at ten o'clock on a weekend night when the central bank is locked up, your vendor's phone still chimes in three seconds.

29
00:03:00,450 --> 00:03:04,010
What makes a rival bank trust your transfer instantly?

30
00:03:05,240 --> 00:03:14,340
The answer sits with the neutral traffic controller in the middle: the national payment switch and its pre-funded net debit cap.

31
00:03:14,340 --> 00:03:23,300
Before any bank can join the 24/7 fast network, it has to lock reserve cash or government bills in escrow at the central bank.

32
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:26,780
That escrow backs a strict intraday debt ceiling.

33
00:03:26,780 --> 00:03:28,270
Watch the clock tick.

34
00:03:28,270 --> 00:03:35,520
At second one, your bank deducts twenty million dong from your balance and fires an encrypted message.

35
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:47,680
At second two, the switch checks that twenty million still fits inside your bank's locked collateral cap, subtracts it from the remaining quota, and stamps the order good.

36
00:03:47,680 --> 00:03:55,230
Backed by that locked collateral, the receiving bank safely hands out twenty million dong at second three.

37
00:03:56,320 --> 00:03:59,230
Freeze right at second four after the chime.

38
00:03:59,230 --> 00:04:06,750
Your supplier checks their phone, sees twenty million dong added, and can spend it on payroll or bills right away.

39
00:04:06,750 --> 00:04:16,120
Their accountant records a Circular 99 entry on the spot: Debit Account 112 for twenty million dong and Credit Account 131 for twenty million.

40
00:04:16,120 --> 00:04:19,350
For both you and your vendor, the deal is closed.

41
00:04:19,350 --> 00:04:25,550
Look between the two banks, though, and zero central-bank reserve money has changed hands yet.

42
00:04:25,550 --> 00:04:36,030
At second three, the receiving bank is advancing usable credit based on the collateral your bank locked up ahead of time, knowing final cash will settle later.

43
00:04:37,330 --> 00:04:43,960
Why don't banks shuttle twenty million dong of central-bank reserves back and forth every three seconds?

44
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:50,970
Picture millions of everyday transfers flying across town all day — morning coffee, groceries, supplier bills.

45
00:04:50,970 --> 00:04:58,750
Suppose during one clearing session, Bank A customers send 500 billion dong over to Bank B, including your twenty million.

46
00:04:58,750 --> 00:05:04,230
Meanwhile, Bank B customers send 470 billion dong flying the other way back to Bank A.

47
00:05:04,230 --> 00:05:07,350
Two-way gross volume adds up to 970 billion dong.

48
00:05:07,350 --> 00:05:14,810
When the clearing window closes, the switch subtracts the 470 billion return flow from the 500 billion outgoing flow.

49
00:05:14,810 --> 00:05:20,290
Almost a trillion dong cancels itself out, leaving a net bill of just 30 billion dong.

50
00:05:21,670 --> 00:05:29,760
That 30-billion-dong net tally goes straight to the central bank, where every commercial bank keeps its master settlement account.

51
00:05:29,760 --> 00:05:39,270
There, the central bank runs one clean reserve entry: it debits 30 billion dong from Bank A's settlement balance and credits 30 billion dong to Bank B's.

52
00:05:39,270 --> 00:05:52,330
By boiling 970 billion dong in two-way payments down to one 30-billion-dong transfer, the banking system spares 940 billion dong in high-powered reserves from ever moving — nearly a 97% cut in liquidity needed.

53
00:05:52,330 --> 00:05:58,610
That massive reserve saving is the hidden math that lets banks give you free transfers day and night.

54
00:05:59,790 --> 00:06:04,800
Two real-world situations step off that 24/7 retail netting track.

55
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:13,380
First, if your company wires a huge sum — say 10 billion dong — that order beats the retail fast-payment ceiling.

56
00:06:13,380 --> 00:06:22,410
It goes straight into the central bank's RTGS system to settle gross reserves one wire at a time during business hours.

57
00:06:22,410 --> 00:06:26,590
Miss the afternoon cutoff, and it waits until tomorrow.

58
00:06:26,590 --> 00:06:34,560
Second, suppose at 16:55 on December 31 you wire 20 million dong and clearing queues stall past midnight.

59
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:41,770
Your bank statement already dropped by twenty million, yet the supplier hasn't received a cent.

60
00:06:41,770 --> 00:06:53,760
Under Circular 99 rules, you park that floating cash in Account 113 as cash in transit, then clear the supplier payable once delivery is confirmed in January.

61
00:06:54,680 --> 00:07:02,320
The next time you tap send on a 20-million-dong transfer and hear the chime three seconds later, you know what both floors are doing.

62
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:09,560
If your partner shares your bank, two ledger rows just swapped places in one room so the bank kept 100% of its cheap deposits.

63
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:19,670
If they bank across town, that chime rode locked collateral at the payment switch, while real reserves waited hours for a multi-billion-dong net settlement at the central bank.

64
00:07:19,670 --> 00:07:24,030
Same 3-second chime on screen — two completely different engines underneath.
